<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charax Spasinou</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charax Spasinou was an ancient port city at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. Founded as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and refounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyspaosines">Hyspaosines</a> around 141 BCE, it was the capital of the kingdom of Characene and a hub of trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the Gulf and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>. As the rivers shifted its role passed to Basra.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charax-spasinou.jpg" alt="Charax Spasinou" width="320" height="158" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hyspaosines.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name Charax, probably from the Greek for a palisaded fort, was given to several fortified Seleucid towns; the city was also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and Antiochia in Susiana. It stood on a large mound, Jabal Khuyabir at Naysān, near the meeting of the Karkheh, the ancient Eulaios, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>. Pliny the Elder described it on an artificial rise between the rivers, once about 1.9 kilometres from the sea with its own harbour, but by his time far inland as river silt pushed the coast back.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> founded the city in 324 BCE in place of a small Persian settlement, Durine, and settled Macedonian veterans in a quarter called Pella. After floods destroyed it, it was rebuilt with an embankment against floods almost four and a half kilometres long. Amid the instability that followed the Parthian conquest of most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyspaosines">Hyspaosines</a> made Characene an independent state in 127 BCE, and Charax remained its capital for 282 years. Its coins point to a multi-ethnic, Hellenised city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> annexed it in 116 CE and watched ships leave for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, wishing he were young enough to follow Alexander; independence returned fifteen years later under Mithridates, a son of the Parthian king Pacoros.</p><p>Ships came from Gerrha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> called the city an emporium, and Pliny names it as a market for rare perfumes and a centre of pearl diving. It was the start of the overland route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>. The Chinese envoy Gan Ying reached it in 97 CE, and the geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-charax">Isidore of Charax</a> came from it. Later Arab histories say that Ardašēr, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, killed the last ruler of Characene and renamed the town Astarābād-Ardašīr. As Maysan it had governors through the fifth century and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> in the sixth, and its mint struck coins as late as 715.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Persistent flooding and falling trade with the West led to the city's abandonment in the 9th century. The mound now lies about a kilometre south of the rivers' confluence, which moved during a storm in 1837. Excavations began in 2016 and found a grid of housing blocks measuring 185 by 85 metres, among the largest known in the ancient world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charax Spasinu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hyspaosines, a Seleucid governor of the head of the Gulf, refounded the flooded city of Alexandria on the Tigris as <b>Charax Spasinou</b> and declared independence around 141 BCE as Seleucid power collapsed.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-characene">Kingdom of Characene</a></li></ul></section>
